# Changelog

All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

## [Unreleased]

## [0.2.0]

### Added

- Fully supplied labelled and optional arguments, including effectful defaults, work across functions, imports, aliases, and functors.
- Constructor-set GADTs support polymorphic payloads, mutable inline records, and unboxed wrappers.
- Polymorphic mutable records support allocation, ownership contracts, reads, and writes through generic functions, modules, and functors.
- Arrays support `make`, `length`, `get` and `set`, with entries owned through `Array.cell a i`.
- Standard-library `ignore` evaluates its argument while discarding its result.
- Standard-library `List.length` is supported in executable code with an overflow precondition.
- Inline `List.fold_left` callbacks support loop invariants, effects, exceptions, and ghost-state proof scopes.
- Modules can `open` other modules and `include` modules, functor applications, and structures, with their proof predicates in scope.
- Companion interfaces check exported module contracts and frozen invariant masks against existing native and instantiated functor contracts.
- Pure function contracts are checked through module views and functor parameters, with logical counterparts usable in specifications.
- Proof-defined modules support type aliases, including nonrecursive aliases, and parameterized manifest carrier constraints.
- Native mutex and condition proofs support polymorphic protected-resource facades, owner-aware locking and waits, notifications, helper effects, and Domain workers with checked lock cleanup in modules and functors.
- Domain spawning and joining transfer resources through polymorphic task facades in modules and functors while keeping task bodies verified.
- Access helpers can declare checked physical atomicity with `[@atomic]` contracts, including through imports and functors.
- Atomic-update operations may omit the operation's own arguments, and `delegate` hands an update to a call.
- One-shot `Ghost.Prophecy` predictions support linearizability proofs with future-dependent linearization points.
- Executable contracts support explicit invariant masks with `opens {…}`, using the same syntax as proof lemmas.
- Invariants can declare with `[@within …]` that they lie beneath another invariant, in proofs and interfaces.
- Proof-outline steps at atomic accesses open and close the invariants of the function's `opens` clause.
- A lemma declared `[@closes opener]` closes, at the end of a proof step, what `opener` opened there.
- `Permission.halve` lets a proof take part of a fraction out of an invariant before a field access.
- Proof outlines support loop invariants, OCaml closure and record anchors, occurrence selectors, and ghost return adapters.
- `cut p` proves `p` and verifies the rest of a function from `p` and variables' values alone.
- Quantifiers and auto lemmas' postconditions accept explicit triggers, as in `forall x [f x]. p`.
- `list_segment s over p` declares a list predicate iterating `p`, with lemmas that take one element out and put it back.
- `--only` verifies a source member or module together with its local helpers and companion proof obligations.
- The OCaml 5 tutorial blocking stack verifies with polymorphic LIFO contracts and Domain handoff clients.
- A generic striped hash-set case study verifies atomic set operations and load-factor resizing under all stripe locks.

### Changed

- Executable integer arithmetic follows OCaml wraparound semantics, including intermediate results and pure functions.
- Polymorphic module predicates can be instantiated at concrete types in predicate definitions.
- Proof-file contracts and lemmas accept unparenthesized clauses, matching interface annotations.
- Companion proof files use the extension `.mlp`; `.proof` files are still read, with a deprecation warning.

### Fixed

- Companion interface predicates remain available through annotation-free source module signatures while preserving opacity.
- Nested generic workers can reference enclosing module parameters without spurious dependency cycles.
- Loops no longer retain changing spatial preconditions as invariant facts.

## [0.1.0]

- Verification of a first-order subset of multicore OCaml 5, including polymorphic functions, algebraic data types, records, exceptions, modules, functors, mutable state, and atomics.
- Functional correctness and separation-logic contracts in `.ml` and `.mli` files, including arithmetic safety, loop invariants, and exceptional outcomes.
- Modular linearizability proofs through logically atomic contracts, shared invariants, ghost-state actions, histories, and helping.
- Automatic invariant reasoning and manual proofs in companion `.proof` files, with ghost state, logical definitions, reusable lemmas, and resource algebras.
- Contracts for selected standard-library operations and imported library interfaces, with automatic discovery of local Dune dependencies.
- Command-line verification with source-level diagnostics, verification caching, and generated Raven output for inspection and standalone verification.
- VS Code integration with bundled toolchains, specification and proof highlighting, contract hovers, views of proofs and generated Raven, and streamed verification results.
- Verified Saturn case studies covering the Treiber stack, Michael–Scott queue with history-based and segment-based proofs, and stack-drain clients.
